IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sca-spent`

-- Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) -- the Q&A layer: the questions people ask, with the seat and the situation as
-- parameters, so that one deployment serves every seat and the question form drops the questions that do not arise.
--
-- SEATS (`my role`, the first input of every exported question, "Which of these describes you"):
--   * the person convicted or charged -- the baseline's seat, whose 13 questions are asked here by calling the
--     baseline's functions in sca-spent.l4;
--   * the employer, principal, organisation, licensing authority, employment agency or anyone else dealing with a person
--     who has an old matter -- whose questions are taken not to relate to a spent conviction (s 27(1)), who may not
--     discriminate over it (ss 18-23), and who may not look it up without lawful reason ($1 000, s 28(1)); Schedule 3's
--     exceptions reach them too (cl 1(2)).
-- A question about another role answers `does not arise for you` without reading any other input. The Commissioner of
-- Police, a District Court judge and the Equal Opportunity Commission are never seats: what they may or must do appears
-- as what it means for the person asking.
--
-- The baseline's own input `my role` (IS A `My role`, the Schedule 3 job, licence or role) is asked here as `the job,
-- licence or role concerned`, so that `my role` is the seat alone.
--
-- SITUATIONS (design D8 of the graffiti encoding): within a role, a question that arises only in a situation takes that
-- situation's fact straight after `my role`, and when it does not hold answers `does not arise for you` without reading
-- anything else. The questions of one situation share one input, character for character. They are:
--   * for the person: `I was convicted` (the five questions of a conviction's kind, period and how it becomes spent,
--     ss 4, 6-11, 32, Schedule 2); `I have applied, or will apply, to a District Court judge to have a serious conviction
--     spent` (the two questions of Schedule 1);
--   * for the employer or other person: `the person was convicted` (whether the conviction is spent).
-- The questions on a matter (what Part 3 protects, disclosure, discrimination, records) take the baseline's `My matter`,
-- which says how the matter ended, and arise in every situation. Every situation input is a BOOLEAN, so no situation
-- helper reads an enumeration through OTHERWISE (graffiti finding 31).
--
-- No exported question takes a DEONTIC or EVENT input. The baseline's records (`My conviction`, `My matter`) are taken as
-- the baseline takes them; for the employer they describe the other person, in that person's words.
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md, "Q&A layer (seat and situations as parameters)".

§ `Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) -- the questions people ask`

§§ `Who is asking, and the shape of the answers`

DECLARE Role IS ONE OF
    `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one`
    `I employ, engage, license or deal with someone who has an old conviction or charge`

DECLARE `Yes or no` IS ONE OF
    yes
    no
    `does not arise for you`

DECLARE `Amount` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    amount HAS dollars IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `Months` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `months` HAS `the number of months` IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `Date answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `from` HAS `the first day` IS A DATE

DECLARE `Kind answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `kind` HAS `the kind` IS A `Kind of conviction`

DECLARE `Route answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `route` HAS `how` IS A `How my conviction can become spent`

DECLARE `Protection answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `protects` HAS `what` IS A `What Part 3 acts on`

DECLARE `Disclosure answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `disclosure` HAS `the answer` IS A `Must I disclose`

-- What the Act says of the other person's answer to the employer's question, from the employer's side.
DECLARE `What the Act says of their answer` IS ONE OF
    `the Act has nothing to act on: no conviction and no charge in court`
    `the Act does not excuse it: the conviction is not spent`
    `yes: the protection does not apply here (ss 14, 15, Schedule 1 cl 1)`
    `yes: Schedule 3 excepts the role from this protection`
    `no: my question is taken not to relate to it (s 27(1))`
    `no: the written law is taken not to require it (s 25(2))`
    `no: the agreement or rule is taken not to require it (s 27(2))`

DECLARE `Their disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `the Act says` HAS `what` IS A `What the Act says of their answer`

-- What asks the person, from the employer's side (ss 25(2), 27).
DECLARE `Where my question comes from` IS ONE OF
    `a question I put to them, or to someone else about them`
    `a written law of Western Australia that requires them to disclose it`
    `an agreement, an arrangement, or a rule of common law or equity, as I rely on it`

-- What the employer or other person is to the person (ss 18-23), from their side.
DECLARE `What I am to the person` IS ONE OF
    `their employer or prospective employer`
    `a principal engaging them as a commission agent`
    `a principal for whom they work as a contract worker`
    `an organisation of employees or of employers they belong to or seek to join`
    `an authority that confers a licence or qualification for their occupation`
    `an employment agency they use`
    `someone else, such as a landlord or a shop, dealing with them`

§§ `Carrying the baseline's answers, and the helpers the new seats need`

GIVEN `it holds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`yes if` `it holds` MEANS
    IF `it holds` THEN yes ELSE no

-- The employer's question, as the baseline's `Where the question comes from`, value for value.
GIVEN `where` IS A `Where my question comes from`
GIVETH A `Where the question comes from`
`where it comes from, as they would put it` `where` MEANS
    CONSIDER `where`
    WHEN `a question I put to them, or to someone else about them` THEN `a question put to me, or to someone else about me`
    WHEN `a written law of Western Australia that requires them to disclose it` THEN `a written law of Western Australia that requires disclosure`
    WHEN `an agreement, an arrangement, or a rule of common law or equity, as I rely on it` THEN `an agreement, an arrangement, or a rule of common law or equity`

-- The baseline's answer to the person, from the employer's side. Every value is listed; the court, bail and
-- application values cannot arise from the employer's three sources, and are carried as one.
GIVEN `answer` IS A `Must I disclose`
GIVETH A `What the Act says of their answer`
`what the Act says of their answer` `answer` MEANS
    CONSIDER `answer`
    WHEN `nothing for this Act to act on: no conviction and no charge in court` THEN `the Act has nothing to act on: no conviction and no charge in court`
    WHEN `this Act does not excuse it: the conviction is not spent` THEN `the Act does not excuse it: the conviction is not spent`
    WHEN `yes: the protection does not apply before a court or tribunal that applies the laws of evidence (s 14)` THEN `yes: the protection does not apply here (ss 14, 15, Schedule 1 cl 1)`
    WHEN `yes: the protection does not apply to a bail decision (s 15)` THEN `yes: the protection does not apply here (ss 14, 15, Schedule 1 cl 1)`
    WHEN `yes: the application must set out all previous convictions (Schedule 1 cl 1; s 14)` THEN `yes: the protection does not apply here (ss 14, 15, Schedule 1 cl 1)`
    WHEN `yes: Schedule 3 excepts my role from this protection` THEN `yes: Schedule 3 excepts the role from this protection`
    WHEN `no: the question is taken not to relate to it (s 27(1))` THEN `no: my question is taken not to relate to it (s 27(1))`
    WHEN `no: the written law does not require it (s 25(2))` THEN `no: the written law is taken not to require it (s 25(2))`
    WHEN `no: the agreement or rule does not require it (s 27(2))` THEN `no: the agreement or rule is taken not to require it (s 27(2))`

-- The employer or other person, as the baseline's `Who is treating me less favourably`, value for value.
GIVEN `what` IS A `What I am to the person`
GIVETH A `Who is treating me less favourably`
`what I am, as they would put it` `what` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what`
    WHEN `their employer or prospective employer` THEN `an employer, when I apply for a job or as an employee (s 18)`
    WHEN `a principal engaging them as a commission agent` THEN `a principal engaging me as a commission agent (s 19)`
    WHEN `a principal for whom they work as a contract worker` THEN `a principal for whom I work as a contract worker (s 20)`
    WHEN `an organisation of employees or of employers they belong to or seek to join` THEN `an organisation of employees or of employers (s 21)`
    WHEN `an authority that confers a licence or qualification for their occupation` THEN `an authority that confers a licence or qualification for my occupation (s 22)`
    WHEN `an employment agency they use` THEN `an employment agency (s 23)`
    WHEN `someone else, such as a landlord or a shop, dealing with them` THEN `someone else, such as a landlord or a shop`

§§ `For the person convicted or charged (the baseline's seat)`

-- Each question calls the baseline's function of the same subject (sca-spent.l4) and restates no rule: for this role,
-- and where its situation holds, its answer is the baseline's, carried in the answer types above.

§§§ `ss 4, 9, 10, 11, 32 -- what kind of conviction, and its prescribed period`

@export What kind of conviction is mine under the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA): serious, lesser, or one that can never be spent (ss 4(2), 9, 10)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I was convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter ended with a conviction, in Western Australia or elsewhere (if it ended without one, or never came to court, the questions about a conviction's kind, period and how it becomes spent do not arise)
      `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and what has happened since (any later conviction, certificate, court order, or its own jurisdiction's law)
GIVETH A `Kind answer`
`What kind of conviction is mine under the Act` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN
        IF `I was convicted`
        THEN `kind` (`What kind of conviction is mine` `the conviction`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export How long is the prescribed period for my conviction, in months, before any later conviction is counted (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 11(1)-(3), (6))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I was convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter ended with a conviction, in Western Australia or elsewhere (if it ended without one, or never came to court, the questions about a conviction's kind, period and how it becomes spent do not arise)
      `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and what has happened since (any later conviction, certificate, court order, or its own jurisdiction's law)
GIVETH A `Months`
`How long is the prescribed period for my conviction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN
        IF `I was convicted`
        THEN `months` (`How long is my prescribed period in months` `the conviction`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export From what day may I apply to have my conviction spent, once its prescribed period has expired (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 6(2)(a), 7(2), 11, 32)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I was convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter ended with a conviction, in Western Australia or elsewhere (if it ended without one, or never came to court, the questions about a conviction's kind, period and how it becomes spent do not arise)
      `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and what has happened since (any later conviction, certificate, court order, or its own jurisdiction's law)
GIVETH A `Date answer`
`From what day may I apply to have my conviction spent` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN
        IF `I was convicted`
        THEN `from` (`From what day may I apply` `the conviction`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `ss 4, 6, 7, 8 and Schedule 2 -- how it becomes spent`

@export How can my conviction become spent, and may I apply now (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 4, 6, 7, 8 and Schedule 2)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I was convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter ended with a conviction, in Western Australia or elsewhere (if it ended without one, or never came to court, the questions about a conviction's kind, period and how it becomes spent do not arise)
      `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and what has happened since (any later conviction, certificate, court order, or its own jurisdiction's law)
      `on` IS A DATE @desc The day you would apply
GIVETH A `Route answer`
`How can my conviction become spent, and may I apply now` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN
        IF `I was convicted`
        THEN `route` (`How can my conviction become spent` `the conviction` `on`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export Is my conviction spent under the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) (ss 3, 6, 7, 8, 30 and Schedule 2)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I was convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter ended with a conviction, in Western Australia or elsewhere (if it ended without one, or never came to court, the questions about a conviction's kind, period and how it becomes spent do not arise)
      `the conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc Your conviction, its sentence and what has happened since (any later conviction, certificate, court order, or its own jurisdiction's law)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is my conviction spent under the Act` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN
        IF `I was convicted`
        THEN `yes if` (`Is my conviction spent` `the conviction`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `ss 3, 12 -- what Part 3 protects`

@export What in my matter does Part 3 of the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) protect (ss 3, 12; Young Offenders Act 1994 s 189(9))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
GIVETH A `Protection answer`
`What in my matter does Part 3 protect` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN `protects` (`What does Part 3 protect in my matter` `the matter`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `ss 14, 15, 25-27, Schedule 3 -- must I disclose it, and may it count against me`

@export Must I disclose my old matter when asked, or does the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) let me answer as if it never happened (ss 14, 15, 25(2), 27, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `the job, licence or role concerned` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `where it comes from` IS A `Where the question comes from` @desc Who or what is asking: a question, a written law, an agreement, a court, a bail decision, or your own application to the judge
GIVETH A `Disclosure answer`
`Must I disclose my old matter when asked` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN `disclosure` (`Must I disclose it` `the matter` `the job, licence or role concerned` `where it comes from`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export May my old matter count against me under a law of Western Australia, such as a licence's good character test (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 14, 15, 25(1), 26, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `the job, licence or role concerned` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `how the law uses it` IS A `How a law of Western Australia uses my record` @desc How the law would use your record: a reference to convictions, a character or fitness test, a court on guilt or punishment, or bail
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May my old matter count against me under a law of Western Australia` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN `yes if` (`May it count against me under a law of Western Australia` `the matter` `the job, licence or role concerned` `how the law uses it`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `ss 17-24 -- discrimination`

@export Is it unlawful for them to treat me less favourably because of my old matter (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 17-24, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `the job, licence or role concerned` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `who` IS A `Who is treating me less favourably` @desc Who is treating you less favourably
      `they treat me less favourably because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they treat you less favourably than someone never convicted, because of the conviction or charge
      `they impose a requirement on me that is not reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they impose a requirement or condition that is not reasonable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Equal Opportunity Commission or Tribunal)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is it unlawful for them to treat me less favourably because of my old matter` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN `yes if` (`Is it unlawful for them to discriminate against me over it` `the matter` `the job, licence or role concerned` `who` `they treat me less favourably because of it` `they impose a requirement on me that is not reasonable`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `s 28 -- looking it up`

@export May someone obtain my old matter from an official criminal record (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 28, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How your matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence
      `the job, licence or role concerned` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `they have a lawful reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person looking has a lawful reason to obtain it
      `they are prescribed and screening me for work with children or people with disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is prescribed under s 28(2) and obtains it under a prescribed screening law of another Australian jurisdiction
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May someone obtain my old matter from an official criminal record` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN `yes if` (`May someone obtain it from an official criminal record` `the matter` `the job, licence or role concerned` `they have a lawful reason` `they are prescribed and screening me for work with children or people with disability`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export What is the most a person can be fined for obtaining my spent conviction from an official criminal record without lawful reason (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 28(1))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`What is the most a person can be fined for looking up my spent conviction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN `amount` (`What is the most someone can be fined for looking it up`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `Schedule 1 -- my application to a District Court judge`

@export Will my application to a District Court judge to have a serious conviction spent be heard in private (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) Schedule 1 cl 3(1))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I have applied, or will apply, to a District Court judge to have a serious conviction spent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied, or are about to apply, to a District Court judge under s 6 (the questions on the hearing and costs, Schedule 1, arise only then)
      `I ask for a public hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ask for the hearing to be in public
      `the judge considers it should be public` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judge considers the hearing should be in public
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Will my application to a District Court judge be heard in private` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN
        IF `I have applied, or will apply, to a District Court judge to have a serious conviction spent`
        THEN `yes if` (`Will my hearing be in private` `I ask for a public hearing` `the judge considers it should be public`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export Can I be ordered to pay costs on my application to a District Court judge (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) Schedule 1 cll 7, 8)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I have applied, or will apply, to a District Court judge to have a serious conviction spent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied, or are about to apply, to a District Court judge under s 6 (the questions on the hearing and costs, Schedule 1, arise only then)
      `the judge refused it without a hearing as vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judge refused your application under Schedule 1 cl 7(a)
      `the judge thinks the circumstances justify costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judge is of the opinion that the circumstances justify awarding costs
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Can I be ordered to pay costs on my application to a District Court judge` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence, or charged with one` THEN
        IF `I have applied, or will apply, to a District Court judge to have a serious conviction spent`
        THEN `yes if` (`Can I be ordered to pay costs` `the judge refused it without a hearing as vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance` `the judge thinks the circumstances justify costs`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§ `For an employer, principal, agency or anyone else dealing with a person who has an old matter (ss 17-24, 27, 28, Schedule 3)`

-- Part 3 binds the other side: a question put about a spent conviction is taken not to relate to it (s 27(1)); an
-- employer, principal, organisation, licensing authority or employment agency may not discriminate over it (ss 18-23);
-- no one may obtain it from an official record without lawful reason ($1 000, s 28(1)). Schedule 3's exceptions
-- extend to the employer or authority on the other side of each role (cl 1(2)).

§§§ `ss 3, 12, 25(2), 27, Schedule 3 -- what my question reaches`

@export Is the person's conviction spent under the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) (ss 3, 6, 7, 8, 30 and Schedule 2)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the person was convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person's matter ended with a conviction (if not, whether a conviction is spent does not arise)
      `their conviction` IS A `My conviction` @desc The person's conviction, its sentence and what has happened since (the fields are worded from their side)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is the person's conviction spent` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I employ, engage, license or deal with someone who has an old conviction or charge` THEN
        IF `the person was convicted`
        THEN `yes if` (`Is my conviction spent` `their conviction`)
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export What in the person's matter does Part 3 of the Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) protect (ss 3, 12)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `their matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How the person's matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence (the fields are worded from their side)
GIVETH A `Protection answer`
`What in the person's matter does Part 3 protect` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I employ, engage, license or deal with someone who has an old conviction or charge` THEN `protects` (`What does Part 3 protect in my matter` `their matter`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export Must the person disclose their old matter in answer to my question, form or agreement, or is it taken not to reach it (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 25(2), 27, Schedule 3 cl 1(2))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `their matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How the person's matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence (the fields are worded from their side)
      `the job, licence or role concerned` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `where my question comes from` IS A `Where my question comes from` @desc What asks the person about their record: a question you put to them, or to someone else about them (a job form, an interview); a written law of Western Australia that requires disclosure; or an agreement, arrangement or rule of common law or equity
GIVETH A `Their disclosure`
`Must the person disclose their old matter in answer to my question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I employ, engage, license or deal with someone who has an old conviction or charge` THEN `the Act says` (`what the Act says of their answer` (`Must I disclose it` `their matter` `the job, licence or role concerned` (`where it comes from, as they would put it` `where my question comes from`)))
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `ss 17-24 -- may I treat them less favourably`

@export Is it unlawful for me to treat the person less favourably because of their old matter (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) ss 17-24, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `their matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How the person's matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence (the fields are worded from their side)
      `the job, licence or role concerned` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `what I am to them` IS A `What I am to the person` @desc What you are to the person: their employer or prospective employer; a principal engaging them as a commission agent or contract worker; an organisation of employees or employers; an authority conferring a licence or qualification for their occupation; an employment agency; or someone else, such as a landlord or a shop
      `I treat them less favourably because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you treat the person less favourably than someone never convicted, because of the conviction or charge
      `I impose a requirement on them that is not reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you impose a requirement or condition on them that is not reasonable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Equal Opportunity Commission or Tribunal)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is it unlawful for me to treat the person less favourably because of their old matter` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I employ, engage, license or deal with someone who has an old conviction or charge` THEN `yes if` (`Is it unlawful for them to discriminate against me over it` `their matter` `the job, licence or role concerned` (`what I am, as they would put it` `what I am to them`) `I treat them less favourably because of it` `I impose a requirement on them that is not reasonable`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `s 28 -- may I look it up`

@export May I obtain the person's old matter from an official criminal record (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 28, Schedule 3)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `their matter` IS A `My matter` @desc How the person's matter ended, the conviction if any, and the kind of offence (the fields are worded from their side)
      `the job, licence or role concerned` IS A `My role` @desc The job, licence, check or role concerned, if it is one of the Schedule 3 roles; otherwise none of the Schedule 3 roles
      `I have a lawful reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a lawful reason to obtain it
      `I am prescribed and screening them for work with children or people with disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are prescribed under s 28(2) and obtain it under a prescribed screening law of another Australian jurisdiction
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May I obtain the person's old matter from an official criminal record` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I employ, engage, license or deal with someone who has an old conviction or charge` THEN `yes if` (`May someone obtain it from an official criminal record` `their matter` `the job, licence or role concerned` `I have a lawful reason` `I am prescribed and screening them for work with children or people with disability`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export What is the most I can be fined for obtaining a spent conviction from an official criminal record without lawful reason (Spent Convictions Act 1988 (WA) s 28(1))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`What is the most I can be fined for looking up a spent conviction without lawful reason` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I employ, engage, license or deal with someone who has an old conviction or charge` THEN `amount` `What is the most someone can be fined for looking it up`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
